[quote="phantaskippy"]

They are hero targets, Legacy produces a static effect that increases damage dealt by hero targets.Basically when something is going to deal damage, activated Galvanize checks to see if it is a hero target, if it is that damage is boosted.Galvanize doesn&#39;t care when the hero target entered play, if it tries to deal damage while Galvanize is active Galvanize raises that damage.

[/quote]

Yes. The +1 from Galvanize is a <em>static</em> buff that affects all <em>Hero Targets</em> (cards that [a] belong to a hero deck and [b] have HP). As long as Galvanize is active, any hero target would have their damage increased.&nbsp;

This is ultimatly a trivial nitpick, but Galvanize doesn&#39;t do a &quot;check&quot; nessesarily. As you said, it has already been activated. Hero targets are still considered to be under the effects of Galvanize whether they are actively doing damage or not. &nbsp;The timing element is unessesary.&nbsp;

[quote="TheJayMann"]

 That said, I don&#39;t think anyone believed this was trigger based, thus this comparision never really needed to be made.&nbsp; Even using words such as &#39;Galvanize checks that the target is a hero target&#39; doesn&#39;t necessarily imply that Galvanize is trigger based.&nbsp; The fact the wording of the power has a filter word at all (hero targets) means that it is specifically a check. &nbsp; 

[/quote]

I do agree that &quot;check&quot; does not <em>nessesarily</em> imply something being trigger based. What &quot;checking&quot; <em>does&nbsp;</em>imply however is an element of timing. It is inherent in the connotation of the word that there must be a point in time where you &quot;check&quot; it and it easily follows that there would be a point where you wouldn&#39;t. This would not be too major of an issue, until you realize that all issues of timing like this on effects are handled by their Triggers. So in a round-about way, &quot;checking&quot; certainly can lead to a logical conclusion of being the equivilent of a Trigger.&nbsp;

If you wanted to say Galvinize &quot;checks&quot; that filter right after its used (to cover hero targets currently in play) and does a quick check for every additional target that enters play while the effect is active, that would seem to be more palletable, but that still is unnessesarily convoluted.&nbsp; There is&nbsp; <em style="font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px;">never</em> &nbsp;going to be  &nbsp;a point where a hero target becomes a non-hero target. It will either always be affected by Galvinize or it will never be. There is no real reason to insert any notion of timing to the effect past its activation.&nbsp;

